{
  "heading": "Who may call a route in every Yon language",
  "axis": "language",
  "body": "Choose a language below. The controller class says `SignedIn`, so any signed-in user may call its routes. `getOrders` says `Public`, so anyone may call it: the rule on a method wins over the rule on its class. `deleteOrders` says `Allow(Admin)`, so only a user with the role `Admin` may call it. A role is a type: an empty class in `server/roles/`, named after the role. The one class marked `IdentityProvider` says who is signed in. Its method `identify` reads the request in the language's own type and returns the user and the names of their roles, or no value when nobody is signed in. PHP has no request object, so its `identify` reads `$_SERVER`. Lua reads the request table, `request.headers[\"authorization\"]`, with the header name in lower case. With the header `authorization: ada-token`, every route answers. With `bob-token`, `DELETE /orders` answers 403. Without the header, `POST /orders` and `DELETE /orders` answer 401. The example keeps its sessions in a constant; a real app checks a token or a session store.\n\nC++ has no tab here, because C++ Yon code has only route controllers so far: the build refuses `IdentityProvider` in C++ with `TY2015` (ADR 0099).",
